All extant contemporary evidence points to the 19th-century lighthouse keeper Imelda Rooksby-Tann as the originator.  Three independent primary sources—
• her signed Pednvarrow Keeper’s Log entry of 14 Nov 1873 [P1];
• the Northern Coastal Lighthouse Service personnel register (administrative note, 1874) [P2];
• an engraved brass plate on the Pednvarrow lantern gallery, metallurgically dated to the 1870s–1880s and bearing both the proverb and her name [P3]—
all associate the phrase directly with her during her period of service and none pre-date her use.  Secondary compilations (e.g., Quotation Encyclopedia [P4], Coastal Heritage Review [P5]) merely repeat these primary records.

Because the attribution rests on multiple, mutually independent, contemporaneous documents and a physical artefact, with no rival claim known, scholarly consensus treats the credit to Imelda Rooksby-Tann as firmly established; remaining uncertainty is limited to the possibility of unrecorded oral antecedents, for which no evidence currently exists.
